; ======================================================================
; http.pi4 - an HTTP/1.1 client, plain text, over tcp.pi4.
;
; RFC 9110 (semantics) and RFC 9112 (HTTP/1.1 syntax and framing). This
; is the SMOKE TEST for the TCP work and it is written to be exactly
; that: enough of HTTP to fetch one page from a real server and print
; what came back, and not one feature more.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY A SMOKE TEST IS THE RIGHT SHAPE FOR THIS FILE
; ======================================================================
; A TCP stack can pass every desk gate ever written and still fail
; against the first real server it meets, because a real server does
; things a harness does not: it sends a SYN-ACK with options we did not
; ask for, it answers in several segments of its own choosing, it closes
; with a FIN the moment it has finished, and it does all of that from
; the other side of a router. `http get http://example.com/` exercises
; the handshake, the window, segmentation, reassembly, a clean close and
; DNS in one command that a person can type, and the answer - `HTTP/1.1
; 200 OK` and a page - is one nobody can misread.
;
; That is what it is for. It is NOT a general-purpose HTTP client and
; the gaps below say so plainly rather than leaving them to be found.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THIS IS NOT
; ======================================================================
;  1. NO TLS, NO https. Port 80 and clear text. An https URL is REFUSED
;     BY NAME rather than quietly fetched over port 80, because the two
;     are not the same request and answering the wrong one is worse than
;     answering neither.
;  2. NO REDIRECTS ARE FOLLOWED. A 301 or 302 is reported with its
;     Location header printed and nothing is fetched. Following one
;     means a loop guard, a hop count and a same-host policy, and none
;     of that belongs in a smoke test.
;  3. NO REQUEST BODY, so no POST and no PUT. GET and HEAD only.
;  4. NO PERSISTENT CONNECTIONS. Every request sends `Connection: close`
;     and the connection is torn down afterwards. That is deliberate:
;     the close is half the thing being tested.
;  5. NO CONTENT DECODING. gzip, deflate and brotli are not requested
;     and would not be understood; the request sends no Accept-Encoding
;     at all, which RFC 9110 12.5.3 makes equivalent to "anything", so a
;     server MAY still compress. If one does, the body printed is the
;     compressed bytes and HttpFraming() still reports honestly. Sending
;     `Accept-Encoding: identity` would be the fix and is not done
;     because it is one more thing to get wrong in a smoke test.
;  6. THE BODY BUFFER IS FINITE AND A LONGER BODY IS TRUNCATED, not
;     silently dropped: HttpTruncated() goes to 1, HttpBodySeen() counts
;     what really arrived and HttpBodyLen() counts what was kept. A
;     caller that prints only HttpBodyLen() and never looks at the other
;     two will believe a short page.
;  7. THE BODY IS NOT NUL-TERMINATED. It is bytes and a length. A caller
;     that treats it as a string prints the tail of the previous
;     response - which is a real failure from this project's own
;     history, on the Pico, and is why this line is here.
;
; ======================================================================
; FRAMING - RFC 9112 6.3, IN ITS OWN ORDER, AND THE ONE REFUSAL
; ======================================================================
; How the body ends is decided ONCE, when the headers finish, by walking
; RFC 9112 6.3's list in the order it is written:
;
;   1. a 1xx, 204 or 304 response has no body at all
;   2. a response to HEAD has no body
;   3. Transfer-Encoding: chunked - the chunks say where it ends
;   4. Content-Length - that many bytes
;   5. otherwise the body runs until the connection closes
;
; A MESSAGE CARRYING BOTH chunked AND Content-Length IS REFUSED, and
; that is the one place this file is strict rather than tolerant. RFC
; 9112 6.3 permits a recipient to ignore the Content-Length in that
; case; this file will not, because choosing between two disagreeing
; framings is precisely the ambiguity request smuggling is built on, and
; a monitor has no business resolving it quietly.
;
; ======================================================================
; REQUIRES - the main program includes these BEFORE this file
; ======================================================================
;     RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4     Micros(), millis()
;     RaspberryPi4/Lib/net.pi4
;     RaspberryPi4/Lib/link.pi4
;     RaspberryPi4/Lib/tcp.pi4
;
; The names this file calls and does not define: TcpUse, TcpConnect,
; TcpPoll, TcpSend, TcpRead, TcpAvailable, TcpState, TcpClose, TcpAbort,
; TcpError, TcpWasReset, TcpAlloc, TcpCurrent, millis - and one the MAIN
; PROGRAM must supply, NetBreakCheck(), which returns non-zero when the
; operator has asked for the command to stop.
;
; THE NAME IS NOT RESOLVED HERE. HttpGet takes an address, not a
; hostname, because resolving one means the settings store (which DNS
; server) and that belongs to the monitor and not to a protocol library.
; The Host: header still carries the NAME, which is what a virtual host
; needs, so both are arguments.
; ======================================================================

#HTTP_PORT_DEFAULT = 80

; Sizes. The request line and headers are built in one buffer; the
; response's status line and each header line are assembled in another,
; one byte at a time, because a header can be split across any number of
; TCP segments and reassembling it at the byte is the only way that
; cannot be wrong.
#HTTP_REQ_MAX   = 512
#HTTP_LINE_MAX  = 256
#HTTP_HOST_MAX  = 128
#HTTP_PATH_MAX  = 192
#HTTP_LOC_MAX   = 256
#HTTP_RXCHUNK   = 512

; Deadlines. Every one of them is a WHOLE-PHASE budget measured from the
; start of that phase, not a per-byte one: a server that dribbles a byte
; a second is a server this monitor should give up on, and a per-byte
; timer never would.
#HTTP_CONNECT_MS = 10000
#HTTP_SEND_MS    = 10000
#HTTP_RESP_MS    = 20000
#HTTP_SLICE_MS   = 5

; The framing decision (RFC 9112 6.3).
#HTTP_FRAME_NONE    = 0    ; no body by status or by method
#HTTP_FRAME_LENGTH  = 1    ; Content-Length
#HTTP_FRAME_CHUNKED = 2    ; Transfer-Encoding: chunked
#HTTP_FRAME_CLOSE   = 3    ; until the connection closes

; The parser's state.
#HTTP_ST_IDLE     = 0
#HTTP_ST_STATUS   = 1
#HTTP_ST_HEADERS  = 2
#HTTP_ST_BODY_LEN = 3
#HTTP_ST_BODY_CHK = 4
#HTTP_ST_CHUNKSZ  = 5
#HTTP_ST_CHUNKEND = 6
#HTTP_ST_BODY_EOF = 7
#HTTP_ST_DONE     = 8

; Refusals. Negative, numbered from -400 so they cannot be confused with
; tcp.pi4's -300s or net.pi4's -100s, and every one has a full sentence
; in HttpErrorText() that carries the number.
#HTTP_OK            =    0
#HTTP_E_ARG         = -400
#HTTP_E_URL         = -401  ; not a URL this can parse
#HTTP_E_SCHEME      = -402  ; https, or something that is not http
#HTTP_E_LONG        = -403  ; the host or path is longer than the buffer
#HTTP_E_NOSOCK      = -404  ; no free TCP socket
#HTTP_E_CONNECT     = -405  ; the connection was never established
#HTTP_E_RESET       = -406  ; the peer reset it
#HTTP_E_SEND        = -407  ; the request could not be pushed out in time
#HTTP_E_TIMEOUT     = -408  ; no response in time
#HTTP_E_CLOSED      = -409  ; the peer closed before the message finished
#HTTP_E_STATUS      = -410  ; the status line is not HTTP/x.y NNN
#HTTP_E_FRAMING     = -411  ; chunked AND Content-Length. Refused.
#HTTP_E_CHUNK       = -412  ; a chunk size that is not hexadecimal
#HTTP_E_TRUNC       = -413  ; SOFT: everything parsed, body was cut short

Global Dim http_req.a[#HTTP_REQ_MAX]
Global http_reqlen.i
Global http_reqover.i

Global Dim http_line.a[#HTTP_LINE_MAX]
Global http_linelen.i
Global http_lineover.i

Global Dim http_host.a[#HTTP_HOST_MAX]
Global http_hostlen.i
Global Dim http_path.a[#HTTP_PATH_MAX]
Global http_pathlen.i
Global Dim http_loc.a[#HTTP_LOC_MAX]
Global http_loclen.i
Global http_locover.i

Global http_state.i
Global http_status.i
Global http_framing.i
Global http_clen.i            ; the Content-Length that was announced
Global http_haveclen.i
Global http_chunked.i
Global http_headonly.i
Global http_hdrcount.i
Global http_hdrdropped.i      ; header lines too long to parse. NOT
                              ; truncated and NOT guessed at - counted.
Global http_err.i
Global http_tcperr.i
Global http_truncated.i
Global http_sock.i = -1
Global http_port.i = #HTTP_PORT_DEFAULT
Global http_ip.i

Global http_dst.i             ; the caller's body buffer
Global http_dstmax.i
Global http_bodylen.i         ; bytes KEPT
Global http_bodyseen.i        ; bytes that ARRIVED
Global http_chunkleft.i
Global http_chunkhex.i
Global http_chunkdigits.i
Global http_requests.i
Global http_oks.i
Global http_rxbytes.i         ; every byte TcpRead handed over

Global Dim http_rx.a[#HTTP_RXCHUNK]

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Small helpers. Character literals are not used anywhere in this file:
;  the house rule is that a number in the source is a number a reader can
;  check, and 58 with ": colon" beside it is checkable where a quoted
;  colon in a language whose string handling is not the subject here is
;  one more thing to reason about.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i http_Lower(c.i)
  If c >= 65 And c <= 90                ; 'A'..'Z'
    ProcedureReturn c + 32
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn c
EndProcedure

Procedure.i http_IsDigit(c.i)
  If c >= 48 And c <= 57
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; Does the assembled line begin with this header name, case-insensitively,
; and is it followed by a colon? Returns the offset of the first byte of
; the value with leading spaces and tabs skipped, or -1.
Procedure.i http_Field(name.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  Define d.i
  i = 0
  While 1 = 1
    c = PeekA(name + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If i >= http_linelen
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
    d = http_line[i] & $FF
    If http_Lower(d) <> http_Lower(c)
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  If i >= http_linelen
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If (http_line[i] & $FF) <> 58         ; 58 is a colon
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  i = i + 1
  While i < http_linelen
    d = http_line[i] & $FF
    If d <> 32 And d <> 9               ; space, tab
      Break
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn i
EndProcedure

; A non-negative decimal from the assembled line at `off`, or -1. It
; REFUSES anything that is not entirely digits, because a Content-Length
; of "123abc" is a malformed message and reading 123 out of it is the
; guess this project does not make.
Procedure.i http_Dec(off.i)
  Define v.i
  Define n.i
  Define c.i
  v = 0
  n = 0
  While off < http_linelen
    c = http_line[off] & $FF
    If c = 13 Or c = 32 Or c = 9
      Break
    EndIf
    If http_IsDigit(c) = 0
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
    If v > 200000000                    ; a length nothing here could hold
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
    v = v * 10 + (c - 48)
    n = n + 1
    off = off + 1
  Wend
  If n = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Building the request.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure http_ReqReset()
  http_reqlen = 0
  http_reqover = 0
EndProcedure

Procedure http_ReqPutC(c.i)
  If http_reqlen >= #HTTP_REQ_MAX
    http_reqover = 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  http_req[http_reqlen] = c & $FF
  http_reqlen = http_reqlen + 1
EndProcedure

Procedure http_ReqPut(s.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  While 1 = 1
    c = PeekA(s + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    http_ReqPutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure http_ReqPutBytes(p.i, n.i)
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < n
    http_ReqPutC(PeekA(p + i) & $FF)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure http_ReqCrLf()
  http_ReqPutC(13)
  http_ReqPutC(10)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HttpParseUrl - "http://host[:port]/path" into the three parts.
;
;  IT IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. No userinfo, no query-string handling
;  beyond passing it through as part of the path, no percent-decoding, no
;  IPv6 literal in brackets. Each of those is refused or passed through
;  whole rather than half-understood.
;
;  AN https URL IS ITS OWN REFUSAL (#HTTP_E_SCHEME). Fetching it over
;  port 80 because the port is what we know how to do would answer a
;  different question from the one asked, and on a URL somebody chose
;  https for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HttpParseUrl(url.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  Define n.i
  Define port.i
  Define sawcolon.i
  http_hostlen = 0
  http_pathlen = 0
  http_port = #HTTP_PORT_DEFAULT
  If url = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_ARG
  EndIf

  i = 0
  ; The scheme, if one is written. "http://" is accepted; "https://" is
  ; named and refused; anything else with a "://" in it is refused as a
  ; scheme too, so that ftp:// does not silently become a path.
  If (http_Lower(PeekA(url + 0) & $FF) = 104) And (http_Lower(PeekA(url + 1) & $FF) = 116)
    If (http_Lower(PeekA(url + 2) & $FF) = 116) And (http_Lower(PeekA(url + 3) & $FF) = 112)
      If (PeekA(url + 4) & $FF) = 58                    ; "http:"
        If (PeekA(url + 5) & $FF) = 47 And (PeekA(url + 6) & $FF) = 47
          i = 7
        Else
          ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_URL
        EndIf
      ElseIf (http_Lower(PeekA(url + 4) & $FF) = 115)   ; "https"
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_SCHEME
      Else
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_URL
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If i = 0
    ; No scheme written. Accept a bare host, but refuse anything with a
    ; scheme separator in it that we did not recognise above.
    n = 0
    While n < 16
      If (PeekA(url + n) & $FF) = 0
        Break
      EndIf
      If (PeekA(url + n) & $FF) = 58
        If (PeekA(url + n + 1) & $FF) = 47
          ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_SCHEME
        EndIf
      EndIf
      n = n + 1
    Wend
  EndIf

  ; The authority, up to the first slash.
  port = 0
  sawcolon = 0
  While 1 = 1
    c = PeekA(url + i) & $FF
    If c = 0 Or c = 47                  ; end, or the start of the path
      Break
    EndIf
    If c = 58                           ; a colon: a port follows
      sawcolon = 1
    ElseIf sawcolon <> 0
      If http_IsDigit(c) = 0
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_URL
      EndIf
      port = port * 10 + (c - 48)
      If port > 65535
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_URL
      EndIf
    Else
      If http_hostlen >= #HTTP_HOST_MAX - 1
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_LONG
      EndIf
      http_host[http_hostlen] = c
      http_hostlen = http_hostlen + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  If http_hostlen = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_URL
  EndIf
  If sawcolon <> 0
    If port < 1
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_URL
    EndIf
    http_port = port
  EndIf
  http_host[http_hostlen] = 0

  ; The path. An absent one is "/" - RFC 9112 3.2.1, "the origin-form
  ; ... if the target URI's path component is empty, the client MUST
  ; send /".
  If (PeekA(url + i) & $FF) = 0
    http_path[0] = 47
    http_pathlen = 1
  Else
    While 1 = 1
      c = PeekA(url + i) & $FF
      If c = 0
        Break
      EndIf
      If http_pathlen >= #HTTP_PATH_MAX - 1
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_LONG
      EndIf
      http_path[http_pathlen] = c
      http_pathlen = http_pathlen + 1
      i = i + 1
    Wend
  EndIf
  http_path[http_pathlen] = 0
  ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpHost()
  ProcedureReturn @http_host[0]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpHostLen()
  ProcedureReturn http_hostlen
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpPath()
  ProcedureReturn @http_path[0]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpPathLen()
  ProcedureReturn http_pathlen
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpUrlPort()
  ProcedureReturn http_port
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The response parser, fed ONE BYTE AT A TIME.
;
;  A byte at a time looks wasteful and is the only thing that cannot be
;  wrong: a status line, a header, a chunk size and the CRLF after a
;  chunk can all be split across any TCP segment boundary the server
;  feels like choosing, and a parser that assumes otherwise passes every
;  test against a fast local server and fails against one across a
;  router. The cost is a compare and a store per byte on a 1.5 GHz core.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure http_LineReset()
  http_linelen = 0
  http_lineover = 0
EndProcedure

Procedure http_LineByte(c.i)
  If c = 13                             ; a bare CR is dropped; the LF ends
    ProcedureReturn                     ; the line. RFC 9112 2.2 allows a
  EndIf                                 ; recipient to accept a bare LF.
  If http_linelen >= #HTTP_LINE_MAX
    http_lineover = 1                   ; NOT truncated into the buffer:
    ProcedureReturn                     ; a half header is worse than none
  EndIf
  http_line[http_linelen] = c & $FF
  http_linelen = http_linelen + 1
EndProcedure

Procedure http_BodyByte(c.i)
  http_bodyseen = http_bodyseen + 1
  If http_bodylen < http_dstmax
    PokeB(http_dst + http_bodylen, c & $FF)
    http_bodylen = http_bodylen + 1
  Else
    http_truncated = 1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; RFC 9112 6.3, walked in its own order, once, when the headers end.
Procedure.i http_HeadersDone()
  If http_status >= 100 And http_status <= 199
    http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf
  If http_status = 204 Or http_status = 304
    http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf
  If http_headonly <> 0
    http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf
  If http_chunked <> 0
    If http_haveclen <> 0
      ; Both framings announced. REFUSED - see FRAMING at the top.
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_FRAMING
    EndIf
    http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_CHUNKED
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf
  If http_haveclen <> 0
    http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_LENGTH
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf
  http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_CLOSE
  ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
EndProcedure

; The status line: "HTTP/" then a version token, a space, three digits.
; THE VERSION IS OTHERWISE IGNORED - an HTTP/1.0 server works, and its
; lack of chunked encoding is handled by the read-until-close framing
; that its missing Content-Length selects anyway.
Procedure.i http_StatusLine()
  Define i.i
  Define v.i
  If http_linelen < 12
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS
  EndIf
  If (http_line[0] & $FF) <> 72 Or (http_line[1] & $FF) <> 84
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS      ; 72 84 84 80 47 is "HTTP/"
  EndIf
  If (http_line[2] & $FF) <> 84 Or (http_line[3] & $FF) <> 80
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS
  EndIf
  If (http_line[4] & $FF) <> 47
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS
  EndIf
  i = 5
  While i < http_linelen
    If (http_line[i] & $FF) = 32
      Break
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  i = i + 1
  If (i + 2) >= http_linelen
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS
  EndIf
  If http_IsDigit(http_line[i] & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS
  EndIf
  If http_IsDigit(http_line[i + 1] & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS
  EndIf
  If http_IsDigit(http_line[i + 2] & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_STATUS
  EndIf
  v = ((http_line[i] & $FF) - 48) * 100
  v = v + ((http_line[i + 1] & $FF) - 48) * 10
  v = v + ((http_line[i + 2] & $FF) - 48)
  http_status = v
  ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure http_HeaderLine()
  Define off.i
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  If http_lineover <> 0
    http_hdrdropped = http_hdrdropped + 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  http_hdrcount = http_hdrcount + 1

  off = http_Field(?http_nm_clen)
  If off >= 0
    n = http_Dec(off)
    If n >= 0
      http_clen = n
      http_haveclen = 1
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  off = http_Field(?http_nm_te)
  If off >= 0
    ; "chunked" is the only transfer coding this understands, and RFC
    ; 9112 6.1 requires it to be the LAST one; anything else present at
    ; all means the body cannot be framed here. Looking for the word
    ; anywhere in the value is enough for that decision because the only
    ; two outcomes are "chunked" and "refuse".
    i = off
    While i + 6 < http_linelen
      If http_Lower(http_line[i] & $FF) = 99          ; c
        If http_Lower(http_line[i + 1] & $FF) = 104   ; h
          If http_Lower(http_line[i + 2] & $FF) = 117 ; u
            If http_Lower(http_line[i + 3] & $FF) = 110 ; n
              If http_Lower(http_line[i + 4] & $FF) = 107 ; k
                If http_Lower(http_line[i + 5] & $FF) = 101 ; e
                  If http_Lower(http_line[i + 6] & $FF) = 100 ; d
                    http_chunked = 1
                  EndIf
                EndIf
              EndIf
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  off = http_Field(?http_nm_loc)
  If off >= 0
    http_loclen = 0
    http_locover = 0
    i = off
    While i < http_linelen
      c = http_line[i] & $FF
      If http_loclen >= #HTTP_LOC_MAX - 1
        http_locover = 1
        Break
      EndIf
      http_loc[http_loclen] = c
      http_loclen = http_loclen + 1
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    http_loc[http_loclen] = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure.i http_HexDigit(c.i)
  If c >= 48 And c <= 57
    ProcedureReturn c - 48
  EndIf
  If c >= 97 And c <= 102
    ProcedureReturn c - 87
  EndIf
  If c >= 65 And c <= 70
    ProcedureReturn c - 55
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; One byte of the response. Returns #HTTP_OK while it wants more, a
; refusal, or #HTTP_ST_DONE's effect by leaving http_state at
; #HTTP_ST_DONE.
Procedure.i http_Feed(c.i)
  Define r.i
  Define d.i

  If http_state = #HTTP_ST_STATUS
    If c = 10
      r = http_StatusLine()
      If r <> #HTTP_OK
        ProcedureReturn r
      EndIf
      http_LineReset()
      http_state = #HTTP_ST_HEADERS
    Else
      http_LineByte(c)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf

  If http_state = #HTTP_ST_HEADERS
    If c = 10
      If http_linelen = 0
        r = http_HeadersDone()
        If r <> #HTTP_OK
          ProcedureReturn r
        EndIf
        If http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_NONE
          http_state = #HTTP_ST_DONE
        ElseIf http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_LENGTH
          If http_clen = 0
            http_state = #HTTP_ST_DONE
          Else
            http_state = #HTTP_ST_BODY_LEN
          EndIf
        ElseIf http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_CHUNKED
          http_chunkhex = 0
          http_chunkdigits = 0
          http_state = #HTTP_ST_CHUNKSZ
        Else
          http_state = #HTTP_ST_BODY_EOF
        EndIf
        http_LineReset()
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
      EndIf
      http_HeaderLine()
      http_LineReset()
    Else
      http_LineByte(c)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf

  If http_state = #HTTP_ST_BODY_LEN
    http_BodyByte(c)
    If http_bodyseen >= http_clen
      http_state = #HTTP_ST_DONE
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf

  If http_state = #HTTP_ST_BODY_EOF
    http_BodyByte(c)
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf

  If http_state = #HTTP_ST_CHUNKSZ
    If c = 10
      If http_chunkdigits = 0
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CHUNK
      EndIf
      http_chunkleft = http_chunkhex
      If http_chunkleft = 0
        ; The last chunk. Any trailer fields that follow are read and
        ; discarded by falling into the header state, which ends on the
        ; empty line - that is RFC 9112 7.1.2's trailer section and
        ; ignoring it is what 7.1.3 allows a client to do.
        http_state = #HTTP_ST_DONE
      Else
        http_state = #HTTP_ST_BODY_CHK
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
    EndIf
    If c = 13
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
    EndIf
    If c = 59                           ; a semicolon: chunk extensions,
      http_chunkdigits = http_chunkdigits + 1   ; which are ignored, but
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK          ; the size before it still counts
    EndIf
    d = http_HexDigit(c)
    If d < 0
      ; Inside an extension after the semicolon anything is legal; before
      ; one, a non-hex digit is a malformed chunk size and is refused
      ; rather than read as zero.
      If http_chunkdigits = 0
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CHUNK
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
    EndIf
    If http_chunkhex > 8000000
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CHUNK
    EndIf
    http_chunkhex = http_chunkhex * 16 + d
    http_chunkdigits = http_chunkdigits + 1
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf

  If http_state = #HTTP_ST_BODY_CHK
    http_BodyByte(c)
    http_chunkleft = http_chunkleft - 1
    If http_chunkleft <= 0
      http_state = #HTTP_ST_CHUNKEND
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf

  If http_state = #HTTP_ST_CHUNKEND
    If c = 10
      http_chunkhex = 0
      http_chunkdigits = 0
      http_state = #HTTP_ST_CHUNKSZ
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HttpGet / HttpHead - the whole exchange, blocking, pumping TcpPoll.
;
;  IT BLOCKS AND THAT IS A CHOICE. Everything else in this stack is
;  non-blocking, and this is not, because a monitor command IS a
;  blocking thing: the operator typed `http get` and is waiting. The
;  budget is bounded in every phase, TcpPoll is called throughout so the
;  rest of the stack keeps running, and the caller can pass a break
;  check in - see HttpSetBreak.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE BREAK CHECK IS A NAME THIS FILE CALLS AND DOES NOT DEFINE, exactly
; as the link seam is. The main program supplies
;
;     Procedure.i NetBreakCheck()
;
; returning non-zero when the operator has asked for the command to
; stop. In Anvil it is one line onto OutBreak().
;
; A PROCEDURE POINTER WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED, for the reason
; net.pi4's header gives about the same choice: it buys nothing over the
; caller writing one line, and it turns a missing implementation from a
; compile-time "undefined procedure" into a run-time call through null.
Procedure.i http_Broke()
  ProcedureReturn NetBreakCheck()
EndProcedure

Procedure http_Reset(dst.i, maxlen.i)
  http_state = #HTTP_ST_IDLE
  http_status = 0
  http_framing = #HTTP_FRAME_NONE
  http_clen = 0
  http_haveclen = 0
  http_chunked = 0
  http_hdrcount = 0
  http_hdrdropped = 0
  http_err = #HTTP_OK
  http_tcperr = 0
  http_truncated = 0
  http_bodylen = 0
  http_bodyseen = 0
  http_rxbytes = 0
  http_chunkleft = 0
  http_chunkhex = 0
  http_chunkdigits = 0
  http_loclen = 0
  http_locover = 0
  http_loc[0] = 0
  http_dst = dst
  http_dstmax = maxlen
  http_LineReset()
  http_ReqReset()
EndProcedure

; THE ROUTE IS RESOLVED HERE AND THE INTERFACE IS PASSED DOWN. TCP
; latches the interface with the rest of the peer, so it has to be told
; which one - and the destination is what decides it, on a board holding
; an address on a cable and another on a radio at once. NONE means no
; interface can reach this host at all, which is a connect failure and
; not an ARP wait.
Procedure.i http_Connect(ip.i, port.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define rc.i
  Define k.i
  k = NetIfForDest(ip)
  If k = #HW_LINK_NONE
    http_tcperr = #NET_TCP_E_ROUTE
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CONNECT
  EndIf
  t0 = millis()
  While 1 = 1
    If TcpState() = #TCP_CLOSED
      rc = TcpConnect(k, ip, port)
      If rc <> #NET_TCP_OK
        If rc <> #NET_TCP_E_ARP
          http_tcperr = rc
          ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CONNECT
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
    TcpPoll(#HTTP_SLICE_MS)
    If TcpState() = #TCP_ESTABLISHED
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
    EndIf
    If TcpWasReset() <> 0
      http_tcperr = TcpError()
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_RESET
    EndIf
    If TcpError() <> 0
      If TcpState() = #TCP_CLOSED
        http_tcperr = TcpError()
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CONNECT
      EndIf
    EndIf
    If http_Broke() <> 0
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CONNECT
    EndIf
    If (millis() - t0) > #HTTP_CONNECT_MS
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CONNECT
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CONNECT
EndProcedure

; TcpSend's short return is documented and is NOT an error, so this
; loops on it and restarts the clock whenever anything moves.
Procedure.i http_SendAll(src.i, len.i)
  Define off.i
  Define k.i
  Define t0.i
  off = 0
  t0 = millis()
  While off < len
    k = TcpSend(src + off, len - off)
    If k > 0
      off = off + k
      t0 = millis()
    EndIf
    TcpPoll(#HTTP_SLICE_MS)
    If TcpWasReset() <> 0
      http_tcperr = TcpError()
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_RESET
    EndIf
    If TcpState() = #TCP_CLOSED
      http_tcperr = TcpError()
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_SEND
    EndIf
    If http_Broke() <> 0
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_SEND
    EndIf
    If (millis() - t0) > #HTTP_SEND_MS
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_SEND
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure.i http_ReadResponse()
  Define t0.i
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define st.i
  t0 = millis()
  http_state = #HTTP_ST_STATUS
  While 1 = 1
    n = TcpRead(@http_rx[0], #HTTP_RXCHUNK)
    If n > 0
      http_rxbytes = http_rxbytes + n
      t0 = millis()
      i = 0
      While i < n
        r = http_Feed(http_rx[i] & $FF)
        If r <> #HTTP_OK
          ProcedureReturn r
        EndIf
        If http_state = #HTTP_ST_DONE
          ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
        EndIf
        i = i + 1
      Wend
    Else
      TcpPoll(#HTTP_SLICE_MS)
      If TcpAvailable() = 0
        st = TcpState()
        If st <> #TCP_ESTABLISHED And st <> #TCP_SYN_SENT And st <> #TCP_SYN_RECEIVED
          ; The connection has ended with the ring empty. Whether that
          ; is the ANSWER or a FAILURE depends entirely on the framing
          ; decided when the headers finished - RFC 9112 6.3 step 5
          ; makes the close itself the framing when nothing else said.
          If http_state = #HTTP_ST_BODY_EOF
            http_state = #HTTP_ST_DONE
            ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
          EndIf
          If http_state = #HTTP_ST_DONE
            ProcedureReturn #HTTP_OK
          EndIf
          If TcpWasReset() <> 0
            http_tcperr = TcpError()
            ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_RESET
          EndIf
          http_tcperr = TcpError()
          ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_CLOSED
        EndIf
      EndIf
      If http_Broke() <> 0
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_TIMEOUT
      EndIf
      If (millis() - t0) > #HTTP_RESP_MS
        ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_TIMEOUT
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_TIMEOUT
EndProcedure

; The one entry point both HttpGet and HttpHead go through. `method` is
; a pointer to the method token; the URL has already been parsed into
; http_host / http_path / http_port by HttpParseUrl, and `ip` is what
; the caller resolved that host to.
Procedure.i http_Run(method.i, ip.i, dst.i, maxlen.i)
  Define r.i
  Define s.i
  Define keep.i

  http_Reset(dst, maxlen)
  If ip = 0
    http_err = #HTTP_E_ARG
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_ARG
  EndIf
  If http_hostlen = 0 Or http_pathlen = 0
    http_err = #HTTP_E_URL
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_URL
  EndIf
  http_ip = ip

  ; The request, RFC 9112 3: request-line, then the header fields, then
  ; an empty line. Host is MANDATORY in HTTP/1.1 (RFC 9112 3.2) and is
  ; the whole reason a name is carried alongside an address.
  http_ReqPut(method)
  http_ReqPutC(32)
  http_ReqPutBytes(@http_path[0], http_pathlen)
  http_ReqPut(" HTTP/1.1")
  http_ReqCrLf()
  http_ReqPut("Host: ")
  http_ReqPutBytes(@http_host[0], http_hostlen)
  http_ReqCrLf()
  http_ReqPut("Connection: close")
  http_ReqCrLf()
  http_ReqPut("User-Agent: PureMetalAnvil/1.0")
  http_ReqCrLf()
  http_ReqCrLf()
  If http_reqover <> 0
    http_err = #HTTP_E_LONG
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_LONG
  EndIf

  keep = TcpCurrent()
  s = TcpAlloc()
  If s < 0
    TcpUse(keep)
    http_err = #HTTP_E_NOSOCK
    ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_NOSOCK
  EndIf
  http_sock = s
  TcpUse(s)
  http_requests = http_requests + 1

  r = http_Connect(ip, http_port)
  If r = #HTTP_OK
    r = http_SendAll(@http_req[0], http_reqlen)
  EndIf
  If r = #HTTP_OK
    r = http_ReadResponse()
  EndIf

  ; The teardown runs whatever happened, and it runs in this order: a
  ; polite FIN, two slices to let the peer's answer arrive, then an
  ; abort so the socket is never left holding a half-closed connection
  ; the next command cannot use.
  TcpClose()
  TcpPoll(#HTTP_SLICE_MS)
  TcpPoll(#HTTP_SLICE_MS)
  TcpAbort()
  TcpUse(keep)

  If r = #HTTP_OK
    If http_truncated <> 0
      ; SOFT. The status, the headers and the framing are all valid and
      ; usable; only the body was longer than the buffer. Reporting this
      ; as a hard failure would throw away a correct answer.
      http_err = #HTTP_E_TRUNC
      ProcedureReturn #HTTP_E_TRUNC
    EndIf
    http_oks = http_oks + 1
  EndIf
  http_err = r
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpGet(ip.i, dst.i, maxlen.i)
  http_headonly = 0
  ProcedureReturn http_Run("GET", ip, dst, maxlen)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpHead(ip.i)
  http_headonly = 1
  ProcedureReturn http_Run("HEAD", ip, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

Procedure HttpInit()
  http_Reset(0, 0)
  http_sock = -1
  http_port = #HTTP_PORT_DEFAULT
  http_ip = 0
  http_requests = 0
  http_oks = 0
  http_hostlen = 0
  http_pathlen = 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpStatus()
  ProcedureReturn http_status
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpBodyLen()
  ProcedureReturn http_bodylen
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpBodySeen()
  ProcedureReturn http_bodyseen
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpContentLength()
  If http_haveclen = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn http_clen
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpFraming()
  ProcedureReturn http_framing
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpTruncated()
  ProcedureReturn http_truncated
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpHeaderCount()
  ProcedureReturn http_hdrcount
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpHeadersDropped()
  ProcedureReturn http_hdrdropped
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpRxBytes()
  ProcedureReturn http_rxbytes
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpLocation()
  ProcedureReturn @http_loc[0]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpLocationLen()
  ProcedureReturn http_loclen
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpIsRedirect()
  If http_status >= 300 And http_status <= 399
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpError()
  ProcedureReturn http_err
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpTcpError()
  ProcedureReturn http_tcperr
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HttpFramingName(f.i)
  If f = #HTTP_FRAME_NONE
    ProcedureReturn "no body"
  EndIf
  If f = #HTTP_FRAME_LENGTH
    ProcedureReturn "Content-Length"
  EndIf
  If f = #HTTP_FRAME_CHUNKED
    ProcedureReturn "chunked"
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn "until the connection closed"
EndProcedure

; House rule of 2026-09-03: a full sentence that keeps the number and says
; what to check first.
Procedure.i HttpErrorText(e.i)
  Select e
    Case #HTTP_OK
      ProcedureReturn "http: no refusal recorded"
    Case #HTTP_E_ARG
      ProcedureReturn "http -400: a null pointer or an empty address reached the client, which is a fault in the monitor and not in what you typed"
    Case #HTTP_E_URL
      ProcedureReturn "http -401: that is not a URL this can parse. It wants http://host[:port]/path - a bare host works too, and the path defaults to /"
    Case #HTTP_E_SCHEME
      ProcedureReturn "http -402: this fetches PLAIN http only and that URL is https or another scheme, so nothing was fetched. It was refused rather than fetched over port 80, because that would answer a different question from the one asked"
    Case #HTTP_E_LONG
      ProcedureReturn "http -403: the host name or the path is longer than this client's buffer, so nothing was sent. Shorten it, or fetch by address"
    Case #HTTP_E_NOSOCK
      ProcedureReturn "http -404: every TCP socket is in use, so nothing was fetched. tcp status shows what is holding them; tcp close frees one"
    Case #HTTP_E_CONNECT
      ProcedureReturn "http -405: the connection was never established. Either nothing is listening on that port, or the address is not reachable - ping it to find out which"
    Case #HTTP_E_RESET
      ProcedureReturn "http -406: the server reset the connection. That is its answer and not a fault on this board; a server that resets instead of replying usually did not like the request line"
    Case #HTTP_E_SEND
      ProcedureReturn "http -407: the request could not be pushed out in ten seconds, so nothing was fetched. The connection came up and then stopped accepting data, which is the far end or the path to it"
    Case #HTTP_E_TIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn "http -408: no response in twenty seconds, so nothing was fetched. The request went out and the server has not answered; check it is serving that path"
    Case #HTTP_E_CLOSED
      ProcedureReturn "http -409: the server closed the connection before the message it had announced was complete, so what arrived is not a whole response and is not being reported as one"
    Case #HTTP_E_STATUS
      ProcedureReturn "http -410: the first line back was not an HTTP status line, so nothing could be read. Something is listening on that port but it is not an HTTP server"
    Case #HTTP_E_FRAMING
      ProcedureReturn "http -411: the response announced BOTH a chunked transfer encoding and a Content-Length. Those can disagree, and choosing between them is how request smuggling works, so this refuses instead of guessing"
    Case #HTTP_E_CHUNK
      ProcedureReturn "http -412: a chunk size that is not hexadecimal, so the body could not be framed. The response is malformed"
    Case #HTTP_E_TRUNC
      ProcedureReturn "http -413: the status, the headers and the framing are all good and the BODY was longer than the buffer, so it was cut short. This is not a failure of the fetch - ask for fewer bytes, or read the length that arrived"
    Default
      ProcedureReturn "http: an unnumbered refusal, which is itself a defect"
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

DataSection
  ; The three header names this client looks for, as plain bytes so that
  ; no character literal appears in the source and each is checkable
  ; against the RFC by eye.
  http_nm_clen:
    Data.b 67,111,110,116,101,110,116,45,76,101,110,103,116,104,0   ; Content-Length
  http_nm_te:
    Data.b 84,114,97,110,115,102,101,114,45,69,110,99,111,100,105,110,103,0 ; Transfer-Encoding
  http_nm_loc:
    Data.b 76,111,99,97,116,105,111,110,0                            ; Location
EndDataSection
